course: dictionary
lesson: 5130
title: Invest: money and effort
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To invest means to put money into something, like a business, shares or a bank account, hoping it will grow and give you more back later. Look at the clues: «An index fund invests in many companies at once.» The words money, companies and shares show that invest is about money. Try swapping in put money into, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Invest can also mean to spend time or effort because you think it will be worth it, as in «invest time in practice». The words time and practice are the clues. Think of phrases like invest in, invest money, invest time and a good investment, and you will see the pattern.
explain: Here is the important catch. Keeping money safe in a piggy bank does not grow it, but investing can grow money and can also lose it. «If a pension owns 1,000 shares and each falls by 30p, it is worth £300 less that day.» So when you read invest, the context may tell you it is risky as well as hopeful.
fact: Invest is a verb: invest, invests, invested, investing.
fact: To invest money means to put it into something hoping to get more back later.
fact: To invest time or effort means to spend it because you think it will be worth it.
fact: «An index fund invests in many companies at once»: £100 spread over 100 companies puts £1 in each, so if one company goes bust, you lose only £1.
fact: Investing can lose money as well as grow it, unlike keeping money safe in a piggy bank.
exercise: To invest money means what? | *To put money into something, hoping to get more back later | To hide money under your bed | To count your coins | To spend all your money at once
exercise: «She decided to invest time in learning the piano.» What does invest mean here? | To waste time on games | *To spend time because it will be worth it | To sleep for a long time | To lose track of time
exercise: «An index fund invests in many companies at once.» Why does spreading money over many companies help? | *If one company goes bust, only a small part is lost | The companies all grow at exactly the same speed | The money can never fall in value | The money is safe in a piggy bank
test: Is investing the same as keeping money safe in a piggy bank? | *No, investing can grow money but can also lose it | Yes, both keep money safe and never lose it | Yes, because both always give more back | No, investing only works for old people
test: «If a pension owns 1,000 shares and each falls by 30p, how much less is it worth?» | £3,000 | £30 | £0.30 | *£300
exam: Which word means to put money into something, hoping it will grow? | treacherous | famished | curt | *invest
